MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello Group,

I have avian point-count data collected using a
dependent double-observer method and wish to test competing hypotheses
between groups of species (similar to DeWan et al 2009) using gmultmix.
Species are grouped a priori into different guilds based on clutch
initiation date and diet:

Site     Species     Obs1     Obs2    CID        Diet
1         X               4           0          Y            Y
1         Y               1           1          N            Y
1         Z                0          0          Y            N
2         Y               3           2          N            Y
...

My
 question is, is it possible to do this in unmarked and if so, how
should the data be structure? For example, I know you cannot do
multi-species analyses, so how do you take the above example that has
multiple rows of data per site and transform it into the appropriate
data frame (again, as in DeWan et al 2009).

From u527d9b3ec7deff2d at groups.google.com  Fri Nov  2 15:07:39 2012
From: u527d9b3ec7deff2d at groups.google.com (Dan Linden)
Date: Fri, 02 Nov 2012 15:07:39 +0000
Subject: Re: Species as Fixed Effect
Message-ID: <Dwb7ejml8bs.3XCwWe5ykQAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Dwb7ejml8bs.9yEWtrWcIUEJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <Dwb7ejml8bs.9yEWtrWcIUEJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/Dwb7ejml8bs/m/3XCwWe5ykQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Species becomes a site-specific covariate, so your new "sites" in the data frame are a combination of the original site and species (e.g., 1X, 1Y, 2Y).

In the multi-species analysis where species is treated as a random effect (typically using WinBUGS), each parameter has a species-specific estimate.  Essentially, that is the same as specifying that each parameter has an interaction with species.  So for your model, you would want each site covariate to interact with species, unless you had a plausible reason for thinking the effect of the covariate was common to all species.

The problem with having each site covariate interact with a fixed effect for species is that you don't really gain anything from the multi-species analysis over running separate models for each species individually, especially if you know how to run for-loops in R.  The multi-species models which treat species as a random effect benefit from estimating fewer parameters and sharing information across species.

-Dan

